WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Chapter 7 computed Orontes's average total cost at $10.81 a jar and its marginal cost at $7.20. Chapter 1 recorded a price of $12.00 and a contribution margin of $4.80. Book 2 put the company's cost of capital at 9.04 per cent and its average assets at $29,058,800. All four appear in this chapter's formulas.
10.1  Three approaches
The outcome names three methodologies and the exam expects them distinguished.
Cost-based pricing starts from a cost and adds a margin. Its merits are that it is simple, it can be applied to thousands of products without market research, it covers costs by construction, and it is defensible to a customer who asks how the price was reached — which is why it is standard for government contracts and for professional fees. Its defect is that it ignores the customer: it answers the question "what do we need" rather than "what will they pay".
Market-based pricing starts from the market. Market comparables set the price at or around what similar products fetch, which is the method for commodities, for retail lines facing a shelf of rivals, and for anything whose price is effectively quoted. The company then has to make the product for less than that price, which is the target costing of Chapter 12.
Value-based pricing starts from the customer's perception of what the product is worth to them. It is the hardest to do and the most profitable where it works: a product that saves a customer $100,000 a year can be priced at far more than it costs to make, and at far more than a comparable product with a weaker claim. It requires knowing the customer's economics, which is why it is common in industrial sales and rare in grocery.
Figure F710-02 sets the three against each other.
Figure F710-02. Three ways to arrive at a price
@table
Approach | Starts from | Asks | Best suited to | Main defect
Cost-based | The company's cost | What do we need to earn? | Many products, contracts, regulated work | Ignores what the customer will pay
Market comparables | Competitors' prices | What do others charge? | Commodities, crowded shelves | Ignores the company's own cost position
Value-based | The customer's benefit | What is it worth to them? | Industrial products with measurable benefit | Hard to measure; needs customer knowledge
@end
In practice the three are used together, and the standard sequence in a well-run company is: establish what the market will pay, subtract the profit required, and manage the cost down to what is left. That sentence is target costing, and Chapter 12 develops it. The rest of this chapter is about the cost-based route, because it is the one the exam asks to be calculated.
