10.2  Pricing from a cost
A cost-based price has two components: a cost base, and a markup applied to it. The exam's questions turn on which cost base is being used, because the same price can be expressed as very different markups.
Four cost bases are used, each with its own markup.
Variable manufacturing cost. The markup must cover fixed manufacturing costs, all selling and administrative costs, and the profit. It is the largest markup and the most flexible base, because it makes the contribution visible.
Total variable cost, including variable selling costs. The markup covers all fixed costs and the profit.
Full manufacturing cost, often called absorption cost. The markup covers selling and administrative costs and the profit. This is the commonest base in practice because the figure is already in the inventory records.
Full cost including selling and administration. The markup covers profit alone.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: four routes to the same $12.00
Step 1. The figures. Orontes's variable cost is $7.20 a jar, its fixed costs are $12,624,000 over 3,500,000 jars, which is 12,624,000 ÷ 3,500,000 = 3.61 a jar, and its full cost is 7.20 + 3.61 = 10.81.
Step 2. On the variable cost. To reach $12.00 the markup must be 12.00 − 7.20 = 4.80 over a base of 7.20, which is 4.80 ÷ 7.20 = 66.67 per cent.
Step 3. On the full cost. The markup is 12.00 − 10.81 = 1.19 over 10.81, which is 1.19 ÷ 10.81 = 11.01 per cent.
Step 4. Read the two together. The same price is a 67 per cent markup on one base and an 11 per cent markup on another. A markup percentage means nothing until the base is stated, and a question that gives a markup without a base cannot be answered.
Check. Note which base is riskier. The full-cost markup of 11 per cent is computed on a fixed cost per unit of $3.61 that depends on selling 3,500,000 jars. If volume falls to 3,000,000, the fixed cost per unit becomes 12,624,000 ÷ 3,000,000 = 4.21, the full cost becomes 7.20 + 4.21 = 11.41, and the same 11 per cent markup gives a price of $12.67. The company would be raising its price because demand fell, which is the circular trap built into full-cost pricing and the first thing to say about it in an exam.
Figure F710-03 sets the bases out.
Figure F710-03. Four cost bases for the same $12.00 price
@table
Cost base | Amount a jar | Markup needed | What the markup must cover
Variable manufacturing cost | 7.20 | 66.67% | Fixed manufacturing, all selling and administration, profit
Full manufacturing cost | About 10.20 | About 17.6% | Selling and administration, profit
Full cost | 10.81 | 11.01% | Profit only
Marginal cost, for a special order | 7.20 | Any positive amount | Nothing; it is a floor, not a price
@end
One more conversion appears constantly and is worth automating. A markup is a percentage of cost; a margin is a percentage of price. Orontes's gross margin of 35 per cent corresponds to a markup of 0.35 ÷ 0.65 = 53.85 per cent on cost, and a markup of 66.67 per cent on variable cost corresponds to a contribution margin of 40 per cent on price. Confusing the two is the single commonest arithmetic error in pricing questions.
EXAM TRAP   Markup against margin
A markup is on cost and a margin is on price. A 50 per cent markup is a 33.3 per cent margin; a 50 per cent margin is a 100 per cent markup.
A full-cost price depends on an assumed volume. Say which volume, and note that the price rises as volume falls — which is the opposite of what a market would do.
A cost-plus price is a starting point for negotiation and a check that costs are covered. It is not evidence that customers will pay it.
