10.3  Cost plus a target rate of return
The fourth outcome of this chapter names a particular version of cost-based pricing, and it is the one that connects pricing to Book 2.
Cost plus target rate of return sets the markup so that the company earns a stated return on the capital the product employs. Instead of asking what margin feels right, it asks what profit the investors require and spreads that profit over the units.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: pricing for a 15 per cent return
Step 1. The capital employed. Orontes's average total assets are $29,058,800.
Step 2. The required profit. At a target return of 15 per cent: 29,058,800 × 0.15 = 4,358,820.
Step 3. The profit a unit. Over 3,500,000 jars: 4,358,820 ÷ 3,500,000 = 1.25 a jar.
Step 4. The price. Full cost plus that profit: 10.81 + 1.25 = 12.06.
Check. The method has one powerful merit and two serious weaknesses, and the exam wants all three. The merit is that it ties the price to the return the providers of capital require, which Book 2's Chapter 8 put at a weighted average cost of capital of 9.04 per cent — so a target of 15 per cent is a deliberate premium over the cost of capital, and the difference is the value the company intends to create.
The first weakness is the same circularity as before, and worse: the price depends on the volume, and now also on the capital employed, which itself depends on the volume through inventory and receivables.
The second is that it says nothing about whether anyone will pay $12.06. A target return is an input to the pricing decision and never a justification of it.
Where the method is genuinely used is in regulated industries — a utility allowed to earn a stated return on its asset base — and in long-term contracts where a customer accepts a cost-plus basis and negotiates the rate of return rather than the price. In both cases the customer agrees to the mechanism in advance, which is precisely what makes it work.
Figure F710-04 compares the two cost-plus methods.
Figure F710-04. Markup pricing and target-return pricing
@table
| Markup on cost | Cost plus target rate of return
The markup is set by | Convention, or what the trade bears | The return required on capital employed
Needs an estimate of | Volume | Volume and capital employed
Price on Orontes's figures | 12.00 at an 11.01% markup on full cost | 12.06 at a 15% return on assets
Used in | General practice | Regulated industries, cost-plus contracts
Weakness | The markup has no economic basis | Circular, and still ignores demand
@end
