10.4  Pricing from the market
Market-based pricing reverses the arithmetic. The price is given by what comparable products fetch, and the company's task is to be able to make the product for less.
Three sources of a market price appear in questions.
Comparable products. What similar goods sell for, adjusted for differences in size, quality, brand and service. This is the retail case and the commodity case.
The competitor's price directly. In a market with few sellers, each watches the others, and a price change by one is answered by the rest. Chapter 11 calls this oligopoly and explains why prices there are often sticky.
The customer's alternative. In an industrial sale, the price ceiling is what the customer would pay for the next best way of solving the same problem — which may be a competitor's product, doing it themselves, or doing nothing.
For Orontes, the market price is close to given. Tahini sits on a shelf beside other tahini; the customer compares jars; and Book 1's finding that the gross margin fell from 37.0 to 35.0 per cent while sales grew is exactly what a market-priced product looks like when a company buys share.
Figure F710-05 summarises when each approach governs.
Figure F710-05. Who sets the price?
@table
Situation | Who sets the price | What the company controls | The relevant chapter
Commodity, many sellers | The market entirely | Cost and volume | Chapters 1 to 9
Few sellers, differentiated products | The company, within limits | Price and position | Chapters 11 and 13
A unique or protected product | The company | Price, subject to demand | Chapters 11 and 12
A regulated monopoly | The regulator | Cost, and the asset base | Rate-of-return pricing, section 10.3
@end
The analytical question in a market-priced business is therefore not what to charge but whether to be in the market at the price it offers, and that question is answered with the tools of Chapters 1 to 9.
Take the test at $11.50 a jar. The contribution would be 11.50 − 7.20 = 4.30, the breakeven would rise to 12,624,000 ÷ 4.30 = 2,935,814 jars, and the margin of safety would fall from 24.86 per cent to about 16 per cent. Whether to accept the market’s price is a cost-structure question, and whether to try to change it is the subject of the next three chapters.
EXAM TRAP   A price floor is not a price
The relevant cost of Chapter 6 is a floor below which an order should be refused. The full cost of this chapter is a long-run floor below which the business cannot survive. Neither is a price, and the gap between the floor and what the customer will pay is where the profit is.
In a market-priced business, cost-plus produces a price nobody will pay, and the right use of the cost figure is to decide whether to be in the market at all.
A price change by one seller in a concentrated market will be answered. Any calculation that assumes competitors do nothing should say so.
