10.5  Choosing and defending a price
Four questions settle a pricing decision, and an exam answer that works through them in order will be a good one.
What does the product cost, on two bases? The relevant cost, as a floor for a one-off, and the full cost, as the long-run floor. Orontes's are $7.20 and $10.81.
What will the market bear? From comparables, from the competitor's price, or from the customer's alternative. For Orontes this is around $12.00, and Book 1 shows what happened when the company tested below it.
What return does the company require? The target-return method of section 10.3 converts the cost of capital into a price, which for Orontes is $12.06 at a 15 per cent target.
What will the competitors and the customers do? A price is a move in a game with other players, which is Chapter 11's subject and the reason a static calculation is never the whole answer.
Three further considerations belong in any serious pricing answer.
Price consistency across customers and channels. A special order at $8.50, a supermarket at $9.40 and a shelf price of $12.00 can coexist only while the three markets stay separate, and Chapter 7 priced the risk that they do not.
The legal position. Pricing below cost to drive out a competitor, agreeing prices with rivals and discriminating between customers without justification are all restricted in most jurisdictions, and Chapter 11 names the cartel case specifically.
The signal a price sends. A low price says cheap as well as inexpensive, and a price cut in a branded market is read as a statement about quality as well as about cost — which is a further reason Book 1's two points of margin were expensive.
