LANGUAGE FOCUS   "Markup"
English markup is a percentage added to a cost, and margin is a percentage of a price, and the two are related but never equal — a point this chapter makes arithmetically and language makes harder. In retail speech markup is often used loosely for both, and a shopkeeper who says "I work on fifty per cent" may mean either. Arabic هامش الإضافة names the markup and هامش الربح the margin, keeping the two apart by the qualifier. French is the clearest: taux de marque is the margin on the selling price and taux de marge is the markup on cost, and French practice is to state which is meant every time. An exam answer should do the same, in any language: write the base beside the percentage.
