Case-style practice: a price for a new line
Orontes plans a premium tahini in a 500 gram jar. Its variable cost would be $5.60 a jar, it would need $900,000 a year of new fixed costs, and the volume forecast is 600,000 jars a year. The capital employed would be $4,000,000. Competing premium jars retail at about $9.50. The board's target return on capital is 15 per cent.
Required. Compute a price on full cost plus a 20 per cent markup, and on cost plus the target rate of return. Compare both with the market and recommend. Under 300 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC10-1  Answer A. A cost base plus a markup.
B is wrong. That is value-based pricing.
C is wrong. That is market comparables.
D is wrong. A share target is a strategy, not a price method.
SC10-2  Answer A. It answers the company's question, not the customer's.
B is wrong. Covering costs is what it does best.
C is wrong. It is the easiest of the three to compute.
D is wrong. It is lawful and common.
SC10-3  Answer A. A measurable benefit makes the method workable.
B is wrong. Grocery prices are set by comparables.
C is wrong. Commodities are priced by the market.
D is wrong. Utilities are priced on allowed returns.
SC10-4  Answer A. 7.20 × 1.6667 = 12.00.
B is wrong. That is the markup in dollars.
C is wrong. That is the full cost.
D is wrong. That is the base.
SC10-5  Answer A. 0.35 ÷ 0.65 = 53.85 per cent.
B is wrong. That is the margin itself.
C is wrong. That is the cost ratio.
D is wrong. This inverts the calculation.
SC10-6  Answer A. Volume determines the unit cost and the price affects volume.
B is wrong. The markup is applied once.
C is wrong. Full cost includes variable costs.
D is wrong. The margin base is a different issue.
SC10-7  Answer A. The markup delivers the required return on capital.
B is wrong. Industry margins are a comparables method.
C is wrong. Matching competitors is market-based.
D is wrong. Covering variable costs is a floor, not a target return.
SC10-8  Answer A. 29,058,800 × 0.15 = 4,358,820.
B is wrong. That is 10 per cent.
C is wrong. This has no basis in the figures.
D is wrong. This misplaces the decimal.
SC10-9  Answer A. 4,358,820 ÷ 3,500,000 = 1.25.
B is wrong. That is the whole price plus the profit.
C is wrong. This misplaces the decimal.
D is wrong. That is the contribution margin.
SC10-10  Answer A. The price is given; cost and volume are not.
B is wrong. Full cost does not set a market price.
C is wrong. No single seller sets a commodity price.
D is wrong. Commodity markets are not usually regulated in this way.
SC10-11  Answer A. 12,624,000 ÷ 4.30 = 2,935,814 jars.
B is wrong. That is the breakeven at a price of 12.00.
C is wrong. This divides by 11.50.
D is wrong. That is actual volume.
SC10-12  Answer A. The customer's alternative sets the ceiling.
B is wrong. That is the seller's cost-based price.
C is wrong. The competitor's cost is unknown and not the ceiling.
D is wrong. Regulation applies to regulated markets.
SC10-13  Answer A. One is short-run and one is long-run.
B is wrong. The relevant-cost floor excludes unavoidable fixed costs.
C is wrong. Full cost includes variable costs.
D is wrong. For Orontes they are 7.20 and 10.81.
SC10-14  Answer A. Separate markets keep the prices apart.
B is wrong. Differing products help but are not the condition.
C is wrong. Secrecy about costs is not the issue.
D is wrong. A constant markup does not keep markets apart.
