Item | Answer
Full cost a unit | 30.00: fixed costs are 2,400,000 ÷ 200,000 = 12.00 a unit, plus variable of 18.00.
Price at a 25 per cent markup on full cost | 37.50: 30.00 × 1.25 = 37.50.
Markup on variable cost needed | 108.33 per cent: the markup is 37.50 − 18.00 = 19.50, and 19.50 ÷ 18.00 = 108.33 per cent.
Margin on price at that price | 52.0 per cent: the contribution is 19.50, and 19.50 ÷ 37.50 = 52.0 per cent.
Required profit at 12 per cent on 9,000,000 | 1,080,000: 9,000,000 × 0.12 = 1,080,000.
Price under cost plus target return | 35.40: the profit a unit is 1,080,000 ÷ 200,000 = 5.40, plus the full cost of 30.00.
What it tells you | The same product is priced at 37.50 by convention and 35.40 by the return the capital requires, and the second figure is the one that can be explained to an investor. Neither says anything about what a customer would pay.
@end
Multiple-choice questions
P10-01  Answer A. Cost plus markup.
B is wrong. That is value-based.
C is wrong. That is comparables.
D is wrong. That is regulated pricing.
P10-02  Answer A. The customer's benefit.
B is wrong. That is cost-based.
C is wrong. That is a floor.
D is wrong. A share target is not a pricing base.
P10-03  Answer A. 18.00 + 12.00 = 30.00.
B is wrong. That omits the fixed costs.
C is wrong. That is the fixed cost per unit.
D is wrong. That adds a total to a unit figure.
P10-04  Answer A. 30.00 × 1.25 = 37.50.
B is wrong. That is a 33 per cent markup.
C is wrong. This subtracts instead of adding.
D is wrong. This has no basis in the figures.
P10-05  Answer A. 0.35 ÷ 0.65 = 53.85 per cent.
B is wrong. That is the margin itself.
C is wrong. That is the cost ratio.
D is wrong. This inverts the calculation.
P10-06  Answer A. Volume sets the unit cost and price sets the volume.
B is wrong. The markup is applied once.
C is wrong. Full cost includes variable costs.
D is wrong. The market price is not an input to it.
P10-07  Answer A. 9,000,000 × 0.12 = 1,080,000.
B is wrong. That is 8.3 per cent.
C is wrong. This misplaces the decimal.
D is wrong. This multiplies by 1.2.
P10-08  Answer A. Regulated industries and cost-plus contracts.
B is wrong. Commodity prices are set by the market.
C is wrong. Luxury goods are value-priced.
D is wrong. Special orders use relevant cost floors.
P10-09  Answer A. Cost and volume, not price.
B is wrong. No single seller sets the price.
C is wrong. Regulators are absent from such markets.
D is wrong. Comparables are the market's doing.
P10-10  Answer A. A one-off, where fixed costs do not change.
B is wrong. Regular pricing must cover fixed costs.
C is wrong. Regulated pricing uses allowed returns.
D is wrong. The long run needs full cost.
Case-style set: a price for a new line
The two cost-based prices. The fixed cost a jar is 900,000 ÷ 600,000 = 1.50, so the full cost is 5.60 + 1.50 = 7.10. A 20 per cent markup gives 7.10 × 1.20 = 8.52. On the target-return method, the required profit is 4,000,000 × 0.15 = 600,000, which over 600,000 jars is 600,000 ÷ 600,000 = 1.00 a jar, giving a price of 7.10 + 1.00 = 8.10.
Against the market. Competing premium jars retail at about $9.50. Both cost-based prices are below it — by 98 cents and by $1.40 — which means the company would be leaving money on the table by pricing from its own costs. That is the characteristic failure of cost-plus in a market where the product is differentiated: the method cannot see the premium the customer is willing to pay.
Recommendation. Price at or just below the market, around $9.25, and treat the cost-based figures as floors rather than as answers. At $9.25 the contribution is 9.25 − 5.60 = 3.65 a jar, which on 600,000 jars is 600,000 × 3.65 = 2,190,000 against $900,000 of new fixed costs — an operating gain of $1,290,000 and a return on the $4,000,000 of capital far above the 15 per cent target.
Two things I would check first. Whether the 600,000 jars are incremental or partly taken from the standard line, since each cannibalised standard jar costs $4.80 of contribution and the break-even cannibalisation is 1,290,000 ÷ 4.80 = 268,750 jars. And whether a premium product priced below the premium shelf will be read as a premium product at all — a price is a signal, and $8.10 on a shelf of $9.50 jars says something the board may not intend.
